    "lawCode": "Amd §17, Chap 576 of 1974; amd §1, Emerg Hous Rent Cont L; amd §2, Chap 329 of 1963; amd §10, Chap 555 of 1982; amd §4, Chap 402 of 1983; amd §46, Chap 116 of 1997",
    "lawSection": "Emergency Housing Rent Control Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S8182\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act to amend chapter 576 of the laws of 1974 amending the emergency\nhousing rent control law relating to the control of and stabilization of\nrent in certain cases, the emergency housing rent control law, chapter\n329 of the laws of 1963 amending the emergency housing rent control law\nrelating to recontrol of rents in Albany, chapter 555 of the laws of\n1982 amending the general business law and the administrative code of\nthe city of New York relating to conversion of residential property to\ncooperative or condominium ownership in the city of New York, chapter\n402 of the laws of 1983 amending the general business law relating to\nconversion of rental residential property to cooperative or condominium\nownership in certain municipalities in the counties of Nassau, Westches-\nter and Rockland and the rent regulation reform act of 1997, in relation\nto extending the effectiveness thereof\n\nPURPOSE OF THE BILL:\nThe purpose of this bill is to extend the provisions of the rent\nstabilization and rent control laws, for an additional seven years,\nuntil June 15, 2018.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\nThis bill would extend the sunset date of the rent stabilization and\nrent control laws until and including June, 15 2018, provided that there\ncontinues to be a housing emergency in the city of New York.\n\nEXISTING LAW:\nCurrently, the provisions of rent stabilization and rent control laws\nexpire June 15, 2011.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nThis is a new bill.\n\nSTATEMENT IN SUPPORT:\nThis bill would extend the provisions of the rent stabilization and rent\ncontrol laws that are set to expire next year.  The bill would extend\nthe sunset provision of the rent regulation laws for an additional seven\nyears, until June 15, 2018.\n\nNew York's rent-regulation laws provide protection to over 2.5 million\ntenants throughout the state. The basis for both rent control and rent\nstabilization is a housing emergency, defined as a vacancy rate below\nfive percent. Recent analysis of vacancy. rates in New York City has\nshown that the number of available apartments has decreased to below\nthree percent, which is well below the five percent threshold necessary\nto declare a housing emergency. The loss of affordable housing units can\nundermine and uproot local communities by forcing working families from\ntheir homes in the neighborhoods they helped create many years ago.\nGovernment intervention is critical, and while the State has passed\nvarious legislation in support of tenants in rent regulated and\ncontrolled units, this bill would provide protection until 2018.\n\nBUDGET IMPLICATIONS:\nNone. This bill could be implemented with the funds currently available\nfor these programs.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n\n",
